Proof: For a point $y\in Y$ , let U be an evenly covered open neighborhood of $f(y)$ in X, so $p^{-1}(U)$ is decomposed into disjoint sheets each mapped homeomorphically onto U by p . Let $	ilde{U}_{1}$ and $	ilde{U}_{2}$ be the sheets containing $	ilde{f}_{1}(y)$ and $	ilde{f}_{2}(y)$ , respectively. By continuity of $	ilde{f}_{1}$ and $	ilde{f}_{2}$ there is a neighborhood N of y mapped into $	ilde{U}_{1}$ by $	ilde{f}_{1}$ and into $	ilde{U}_{2}$ by $	ilde{f}_{2}$ . If $	ilde{f}_{1}(y)
eq	ilde{f}_{2}(y)$ then $	ilde{U}_{1}
eq	ilde{U}_{2}$ , hence $	ilde{U}_{1}$ and $	ilde{U}_{2}$ are disjoint and $	ilde{f}_{1}
eq	ilde{f}_{2}$ throughout the neighborhood N . On the other hand, if $	ilde{f}_{1}(y)=	ilde{f}_{2}(y)$ then $	ilde{U}_{1}=	ilde{U}_{2}$ so $	ilde{f}_{1}=	ilde{f}_{2}$ on N since $p	ilde{f}_{1}=p	ilde{f}_{2}$ and p is injective on $	ilde{U}_{1}=	ilde{U}_{2}$ . Thus the set of points where $	ilde{f}_{1}$ and $	ilde{f}_{2}$ agree is both open and closed in Y.
